      <h1 class="article__title">
        Which Countries Use the 90/180 Rule? (It's Not Just Schengen)
      </h1>

      <p class="article__meta">October 21, 2026 · 7 min read</p>

      <p class="article__audience">
        <strong>This post is for you if:</strong> you're thinking about
        leaving the Schengen Area for a few months to let your days age
        out, and you want to know whether the next country has limits of
        its own.
        <br/>
        <br/>
        <strong>It's probably not necessary if:</strong> you're only
        visiting Europe for a single short trip well under 90 days.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article__body">
      <p>
        You're on your 85th day in the Schengen Area and you're preparing to leave.
        Your plan is simple: cross into a neighbouring country, spend a few months there, 
        and come back once enough of your Schengen days have aged out. Plenty of
        long-term travelers do exactly this.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a catch, though. The country you enter may have a 90/180
        rule of its own. Leaving the Schengen Area stops your Schengen
        count, yes, but it doesn't mean your next three months are without 
        the risk of overstay somewhere else.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Visa-free regimes across Europe</h2>

      <p>
        The 90/180 rule is best known as the Schengen Area's rule, but it
        isn't exclusive to Schengen. Eight European countries outside the Schengen
        Area use the same limit for most visa-free visitors, while others 
        use a different model entirely, such as a fixed allowance per visit.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each of these countries tracks separate counts using the same rule. 
        Days in Türkiye don't count toward your Schengen total, 
        and Schengen days don't count toward your Türkiye total. 
        There are two independent counters, each with its own rolling window.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every 90/180 counter works the same way. On any given day, look
        back 180 days and add up the days you spent in that country (or
        area). The total can't exceed 90. If you haven't read it yet, our
        <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window"
          >walkthrough of the rolling window</a
        > covers this in detail.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>A warning for longterm travelers in countries with the 90/180 rule</h2>
      <p>
        There is a lot of advice online that suggests something along the lines of 
        "Spend 90 days in Schengen, 90 days elsewhere. Repeat indefinitely"
        This is known as the Schengen Shuffle, and while it works, it cannot be accomplished
        using only a single non-Schengen country. In "90 days elsewhere", elsewhere is not a single place.
        Not, at least, if it follows a 90/180 rule. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Bear in mind that when you cross directly from one jurisdiction into another,
        <a href="/blog/does-your-entry-day-count-as-a-schengen-day">
        your crossing counts as a full day in both regions</a> at
        once. This actually leads to an important corner case we will cover next.
      </p>
      <p>
        Imagine: You spend 90 days in the Schengen Area, and cross directly
        into Türkiye on your last possible day.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Days 1 to 89: You're in the Schengen Area. Your Schengen counter is nearly
          full at 89; your Türkiye counter sits at 0.
        </li>
        <li>
          Day 90: Today is the last possible day you can exit Schengen without overstaying.
          It is also your Türkiye entry day, since you cross straight from one into the other. 
          Your Schengen count is 90 and Türkiye is 1.
        </li>
        <li>
          Days 90 to 178: you're in Türkiye. By day 178, your Türkiye
          counter is nearly full at 89 days spent in the country. You must leave tomorrow.
        </li>
        <li>
          Day 179: You've used your full Türkiye allowance and must leave. 
          Unfortunately, you can't return to Schengen, as that count is still 90 out of 90.
          That window is still full for another two days, until day 181. To return today would mean an overstay.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        To solve this scenario, you must visit a third region, 
        independent of both Schengen and Türkiye for at least two days.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your intuition might suggest that staying less than 90 days in either jurisdiction
        will fix things. This is wrong, but you're welcome to do the math for yourself. 
        The fact is, in any 180-day period, it takes at least three independent jurisdictions
        to properly shuffle about. It can only be accomplished with two when the non-Schengen jurisdiction
        allows stays longer than 90 days.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Which European countries use a 90/180 rule?</h2>

      <p>
        The list below covers every European destination tracked by EuroVisaCalculator
        with a 90/180 rule for visa-free travelers. It applies
        to most visa-free passports; your own passport may get a
        different allowance or need a visa, so check your nationality in
        the calculator before you plan around it. We keep this list
        current as sources change; see <a href="/blog/how-we-source-and-verify-visa-data"
          >how we source and verify our visa data</a
        >.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A02018R1806-20251230" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Schengen Area (29 countries)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://punetejashtme.gov.al/en/informacione-mbi-regjimin-e-vizave-te-shtetasve-te-huaj/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Albania</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.mvp.gov.ba/en/vize" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.cy/en/information/visas/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cyprus</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ambasadat.net/visas/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kosovo</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.me/en/diplomatic-missions/embassies-and-consulates-of-montenegro" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Montenegro</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://mfa.gov.mk/en-GB/konzularni-uslugi/dali-ti-e-potrebna-viza" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Macedonia</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://mfa.gov.rs/en/citizens/travel-serbia/visa-regime" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Serbia</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.mfa.gov.tr/visa-information-for-foreigners.en.mfa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Türkiye</a></li>
      </ul>

      <hr />

      <h2>Countries that don't use 90/180</h2>

      <p>
        Not every non-Schengen country counts your days the same way.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="rule-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Country</th>
              <th scope="col">Rule</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://www.mfa.am/en/visafreelist" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Armenia</a></td>
              <td>180 days in any 365-day period</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://mfa.gov.by/en/visa/freemove/airport/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Belarus</a></td>
              <td>Up to 30 days per visit and 90 days per 365-day period</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://geoconsul.gov.ge/en/entering-georgia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia</a></td>
              <td>Up to 365 days per visit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://www.irishimmigration.ie/visa-non-visa-required-nationalities/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ireland</a></td>
              <td>Up to 90 days per visit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://www.gov.uk/standard-visitor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">United Kingdom</a></td>
              <td>Up to 6 months per visit</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Armenia</strong> uses a rolling window too, but with
        different numbers: 180 days within any 365-day period, twice the
        days within twice the window. The structure is the same as
        Schengen's, just more generous.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Belarus</strong> doesn't use a single rule. The default
        position is visa-required, and that's where most US passport
        holders land. Canadian passport holders get a narrower visa-free
        entry limited to a handful of named airports, with a 30-day-per-
        visit cap stacked underneath a separate 90-day limit per calendar
        year. Both caps apply at once, so your actual limit is whichever
        one you hit first.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Georgia</strong> gives most visa-free visitors, including
        both US and Canadian passport holders, a flat 365-day allowance
        per visit.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Ireland</strong> uses a 90-day-per-visit limit, not a
        rolling window. The allowance resets when you leave and formally
        re-enter, but Irish immigration authorities have stated directly
        that it isn't possible to spend 90 days in Ireland and then
        immediately re-enter for another 90. Repeat maximum-length stays
        are subject to officer discretion at the border.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The United Kingdom</strong> gives most visa-free
        visitors, including US and Canadian passport holders, up to 6
        months (180 days) per visit under its Standard Visitor rules, not
        a rolling window. Both nationalities need to apply for an ETA
        before traveling. The allowance resets on each re-entry, but UK
        guidance warns that repeated maximum-length stays with only short
        gaps between them can lead Border Force to conclude you're using
        the visitor route as de facto residence, something that's
        assessed case by case and isn't something a calculator can
        predict.
      </p>

      <p>
        A per-visit limit can look more generous than 90/180, because it
        starts over each time you enter. In practice, border officers in
        these countries can still refuse entry to someone who appears to
        be living there on a string of visitor stays.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Your passport changes the answer</h2>

      <p>
        Everything above describes the most permissive regmie/passport 
        combinations. Some nationalities get longer allowances in particular 
        countries, some get shorter ones, and some need a visa where others 
        don't.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's why a single list like this can only be a starting point.
        Enter your own passport in the calculator to see the rule that
        actually applies to you in each country.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Tracking more than one counter</h2>

      <p>
        Tracking one rolling window by hand is hard enough. Tracking two
        or three at once, especially when you're moving back and forth
        between them, is where mistakes happen. Adding additional travelers,
        or passports further complicates matters. Which is what we're here
        to solve. For free. For as long as you need.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/">EuroVisaCalculator</a> tracks each region's counter
        separately, using the specific rule for your passport. Enter your trips
        once to see how many days you have left in each place, and
        when you can revisit.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <h3>Do days in non-Schengen countries count toward my Schengen 90 days?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Only days spent inside the Schengen Area count toward your
        Schengen total. But many non-Schengen countries run a separate
        limit of their own using the same 90/180 rule.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I spend 90 days in the Schengen Area and then 90 days in a neighbouring country, indefinitely?</h3>
      <p>
        This is called the Schengen Shuffle. 
        If the neighbouring country also uses a 90-in-180-day rolling calculation, no.
        You will overstay in at least one location before 180 days is up. 
        There always needs to be a third country involved, or the stay in the neighbouring
        country must exceed 90 days, which is possible in the UK, Georgia and Armenia.
      </p>

      <h3>Is the UK part of the 90/180 rule?</h3>
      <p>
        No. The UK gives most visa-free visitors, including US and
        Canadian passport holders, up to 6 months (180 days) per visit
        under its Standard Visitor rules, not a 90/180 rolling window. UK
        days don't count toward your Schengen total.
      </p>

      <h3>Is Ireland part of the 90/180 rule?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Ireland allows most visa-free visitors, including US and
        Canadian passport holders, up to 90 days per visit, not a rolling
        window. Ireland is in the EU but not the Schengen Area, so its 
        days don't count toward your Schengen total.
      </p>

      <h3>Does leaving the Schengen Area reset my 90 days?</h3>
      <p>
        No. Leaving pauses your Schengen count, but the 180-day window
        keeps moving. Your days come back gradually as they age out. See
        <a href="/blog/when-does-schengen-reset">When does Schengen reset?</a>
      </p>

      <h3>Which countries are actually in the Schengen Area?</h3>
      <p>
        The Schengen Area currently has 29 member countries, covering
        most of the EU plus Iceland, Liechtenstein, Norway, and
        Switzerland.
      </p>

